 ## Overview
-This method reconstructs higher-order symmetric second-order tensors from Diffusion Tensor Imaging (DTI) data by fitting measured gradient magnitudes to a spherical harmonic basis using least squares [S1]. It addresses the limitation of standard second-order tensor reconstruction by allowing the representation of complex, multi-fiber diffusion patterns through higher-order spherical harmonics (e.g., order 4 or 6) [S1]. Unlike standard DTI which yields a single 3x3 symmetric tensor per voxel, this approach models the angular dependency of the apparent diffusion coefficient (ADC) more flexibly, enabling the definition of higher-order tensor lines for visualization [S1]. It differs from [Apparent Diffusion Coefficient (ADC)](12151-apparent-diffusion-coefficient-adc.md) by capturing directional complexity beyond the principal eigenvector, and from standard [Diffusion Tensor Imaging (DTI) Visualization](5713-diffusion-tensor-imaging-dti-visualization.md) by utilizing spherical harmonics rather than direct tensor eigen-decomposition for the reconstruction step.
+This method reconstructs higher-order symmetric second-order tensors from Diffusion Tensor Imaging (DTI) data by fitting measured gradient magnitudes to a spherical harmonic basis using least squares [S1]. It addresses the limitation of standard second-order tensor reconstruction by allowing the representation of complex, multi-fiber diffusion patterns through higher-order spherical harmonics (e.g., order 4 or 6) [S1]. Unlike standard DTI which yields a single 3x3 symmetric tensor per voxel, this approach models the angular dependency of the apparent diffusion coefficient (ADC) more flexibly, enabling the definition of higher-order tensor lines for visualization [S1]. It differs from [Apparent Diffusion Coefficient (ADC)](12151-apparent-diffusion-coefficient-adc.md) by capturing directional complexity beyond the principal eigenvector [G], and from standard [Diffusion Tensor Imaging (DTI) Visualization](5713-diffusion-tensor-imaging-dti-visualization.md) by utilizing spherical harmonics rather than direct tensor eigen-decomposition for the reconstruction step [G].
 
